<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; color: white; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Future Exploration of Space – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Robots on Earth and in Space</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Future Exploration of Space</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify articles for easier understanding. Text: Paraphrase short passages in a simple fashion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify articles on robotics, using diagrams to show main ideas (e.g., points to the diagram and says, "This robot is used in Panama's coffee farms to help sort coffee beans efficiently.")
                Can paraphrase key points on space exploration, using visuals and gestures (e.g., uses gestures to show how a robot collects samples on Mars and says, "The robot uses its arm to pick up rocks.")
                Can use both verbal and visual cues to clarify technical terms for peers (e.g., points to the image of a robot and explains, "Automation means a machine.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows two images: one of a complex robotic arm used in a factory (industry) and another of a simple robot vacuum cleaner. Students are asked to explain the main purpose of each robot in one simple sentence to a partner. The teacher elicits a few responses from the class, focusing on simple language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher projects a short, somewhat technical sentence about space exploration, such as: "The Perseverance rover extensively analyzes Martian geological formations, identifying potential biosignatures through spectrometric data acquisition." The teacher then models how to simplify it: "The teacher points to the image of the rover and says, 'This robot (rover) collects information about rocks (geological formations) on Mars (Martian). It looks for signs of life (biosignatures) using special tools (spectrometric data).' The teacher emphasizes using gestures and pointing to clarify."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I explain the robot's function in a way that is easy to understand?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main idea of the original sentence, but in simpler words?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Robots on Earth and in Space")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students are provided with a short article (approx. 200 words) titled "Robotic Explorers: Unveiling the Universe." The article discusses how robots will have transformed space exploration by 2050, mentioning missions, data collection, and repair of satellites. It includes sentences with future perfect, passive voice, and zero conditional. (e.g., "By 2050, autonomous robots will have explored distant galaxies. Data is collected by sophisticated sensors. If a robot detects an anomaly, it sends a signal.") It also includes some technical vocabulary (e.g., galaxy, orbit, equipment, sensor, satellite).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will participate in discussions on the use of robotics in space exploration and encourage respectful dialogue regarding the future impacts of this technology.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main idea of the article about robots in space?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> Students read 3-4 statements based on the article and determine if they are true or false (e.g., "By 2040, humans will have explored every planet in our galaxy." - False).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match technical terms from the article (e.g., 'sensor', 'orbit', 'automation') with their simple definitions.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice simplifying and paraphrasing information about space robotics using future perfect, passive voice, and zero conditional. They will focus on vocabulary like <i>robot, technology, automation, space, exploration, mission, collect, operate</i>, and adjective <i>automated</i>. Pronunciation practice for /r/ in "robots" and /s/ in "space", and -ed endings for "controlled" (/d/), "launched" (/t/), "operated" (/ɪd/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Simplification:</b> The teacher provides 3-4 complex sentences from the article (or similar examples) incorporating target grammar. Students work individually to rewrite them in simpler language for a general audience.
                        <br><i>Example 1:</i> "By 2040, advanced robotic probes will have meticulously collected atmospheric data from exoplanets." -> "By 2040, robots will have gathered information about the air on planets far away." (Future perfect)
                        <br><i>Example 2:</i> "Critical repairs on space stations are frequently executed by specialized automated equipment." -> "Special machines often fix things on space stations." (Passive voice)
                        <br><i>Example 3:</i> "If planetary gravity is insufficient, objects launched from the surface will not achieve stable orbit." -> "If gravity on a planet is too weak, things sent from it won't stay in space." (Zero conditional)
                        <br>The teacher models pronunciation of key words like "robots" (/r/), "space" (/s/), and "operated" (/ɪd/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Paraphrasing Definitions:</b> In pairs, students receive technical terms (e.g., 'automation', 'satellite', 'gravity', 'exploration'). One student reads the term and its original definition from the article or a provided glossary. The other student must paraphrase the definition in their own simple words, using a gesture or visual cue (e.g., drawing a quick sketch, making a hand motion for 'orbit'). They then swap roles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Explain an Image:</b> Students are given various images related to robots in space (e.g., a robot collecting samples on Mars, a robot repairing a satellite, a future space station). In pairs, one student chooses an image and explains its function or significance to their partner, focusing on simplifying the concept, using gestures, and incorporating at least one future perfect or passive voice sentence. The partner provides feedback on clarity and simplicity.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Space Robotics Podcast Segment (Group Work for 21st Century Skills Project).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Task Description:</b> Students work in groups of three to prepare a 2-minute segment of their "Space Robotics Podcast." Each group chooses a specific aspect of future space robotics (e.g., 'Robots on Mars', 'Satellite Repair Robots', 'Asteroid Mining Automation'). Within the group:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Role 1 (Expert Researcher):</b> Presents complex information about the chosen topic, using specific technical terms (e.g., 'automation', 'sensor', 'orbit', 'gravity') and target grammar (future perfect for speculation, passive voice for facts, zero conditional for functions). They should aim for accuracy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Role 2 (Podcast Host/Mediator):</b> Listens to the 'Expert Researcher'. Their primary job is to simplify and paraphrase the technical information for the "podcast audience" (Role 3), using clear, simple language, and incorporating visual cues or gestures to clarify terms (e.g., pointing to a diagram they've sketched, using hand movements to describe 'orbit' or 'collect'). They might say, "So, what you mean is, by 2040, these robots will have found new planets, right?" or "The robot uses its arm to pick up rocks, like this." (with gesture). This student also ensures respectful dialogue about the impacts of robotics.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Role 3 (Podcast Audience/Questioner):</b> Asks clarifying questions to the Host/Mediator, checking if the information is truly simplified and easy to understand. They might also offer solutions for potential challenges discussed.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will practice their segment, focusing on seamless mediation of information and clear pronunciation of target sounds (/r/, /s/, -ed endings) and vocabulary. They will also actively participate in discussions and encourage diverse perspectives on the future of robotics.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their 2-minute podcast segment to the class. The teacher uses an observation checklist, and peers use a simplified rubric to assess the mediation skills of the 'Podcast Host/Mediator' role, as well as the overall group's collaboration.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Rubric Focus):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of simplified explanations and paraphrasing (Host/Mediator).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of visuals and gestures to clarify technical terms (Host/Mediator).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of target grammar (future perfect, passive voice, zero conditional).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of recommended vocabulary (e.g., <i>robot, technology, space, exploration, automation, collect, operate</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of /r/ in "robots," /s/ in "space," and -ed endings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and respectful dialogue within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short reflection:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one challenging technical term you had to simplify today, and how did you do it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did you use gestures or visuals to help your peers understand complex ideas?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now in simplifying an article or paraphrasing information for someone who knows less about robotics or space?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one strategy I learned today to make complex ideas easier for others to understand?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which mediation activities worked best to help students simplify and paraphrase effectively?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students who struggled with clarifying technical terms or integrating the target grammar?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did the podcast project integrate the mediation skill and the linguistic competences?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to overall learning:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "Robots on Earth and in Space," allowing students to apply all previously learned vocabulary and grammar in a communicative, project-based context. The mediation skills practiced are vital for future academic and professional interactions, enabling them to convey complex information clearly to diverse audiences.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>